# Shell scripts must keep Unix line endings, whatever machine they were written on.
#
# Without this, a script written on Windows arrives on the Linux runner with a
# carriage return at the end of every line, and the very first line becomes
# "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r" - a program that does not exist. The error you get is
# the famously unhelpful:
#
#     bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M: no such file or directory
#
# One line of configuration prevents it for all 50 of you. This is what people
# mean by "fix the reason it was easy to break".
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
